<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<sdf version="1.9">
  <world name="bar_world">
    <physics name="1ms" type="ignored">
      <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
      <!-- Bullet rather than DART's default FCL narrowphase. This is the
           single biggest lever on how long a pour takes, and it costs nothing
           in fidelity.

           Every link of the arm and gripper has a triangle-mesh collision.
           Under FCL, the moment the gripper takes the bottle's weight the
           real-time factor collapses from 1.0 to ~0.1, so trajectories that
           are timed at 0.6-1.7s take 5-12s of wall clock. It is specifically
           the loaded mesh contact: clamping on the bottle while it still rests
           on the counter stays at RTF 1.0, and it recovers the instant the
           bottle is released.

           Measured over 4 pours each, same code, only this setting changed:
             FCL     87.4 / 86.1 / 79.9 / 79.8 s
             bullet  24.6 / 24.2 / 24.3 s
           Both 4/4 with the bottle returned upright and within 8mm of its
           station, so this buys ~3.4x for free.

           Two things that did NOT work, for whoever tries next: halving the
           step count (max_step_size 0.001 -> 0.002) changed the ratio not at
           all, and raising the joint acceleration limits made the planned
           trajectories shorter but the execution no faster - faster motion
           just makes the contact solve harder by the same factor, and it
           starts throwing the bottle. -->
      <dart>
        <collision_detector>bullet</collision_detector>
      </dart>
    </physics>

    <plugin filename="gz-sim-physics-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Physics"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-user-commands-system" name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system" name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-sensors-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors">
      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-contact-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Contact"/>

    <light type="directional" name="sun">
      <cast_shadows>true</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 10 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
      <attenuation>
        <range>1000</range>
        <constant>0.9</constant>
        <linear>0.01</linear>
        <quadratic>0.001</quadratic>
      </attenuation>
      <direction>-0.5 0.1 -0.9</direction>
    </light>

    <model name="ground_plane">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry>
            <plane>
              <normal>0 0 1</normal>
              <size>100 100</size>
            </plane>
          </geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry>
            <plane>
              <normal>0 0 1</normal>
              <size>100 100</size>
            </plane>
          </geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.4 0.4 0.4 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ===================== THE BAR TOP =====================

         Counter top surface is at world z = 0.9 (legs stand on the ground
         plane at z=0). BOTH robot bases and everything standing on the top
         are at that height, which is the coincidence the whole layout is
         built on: a z in either arm's frame is "height above the counter".

         The top is 1.76 (x) by 1.6 (y) centred at (0.08, 0), so it runs
         x -0.80..0.96 and y -0.80..0.80. It used to be 1.2 by 0.6 with arm
         B standing beside it on its own pedestal; the pedestal is gone and
         both arms are bolted to this top, which is most of why it grew.

         WHAT IS ON IT. Every number here is restated in
         bartender_open/layout.py, which derives the slots from a pitch
         rather than listing them, and test_layout.py compares the two file
         against file.

           the arms      A at (-0.45, -0.40) facing +x, B at (0.61, 0.40)
                         facing -x. They FACE EACH OTHER down the bar with
                         the bottle line between them. Arm B's yaw is the
                         one number here that took measuring rather than
                         choosing; layout.APPROACH_WINDOW has the table of
                         readings and the reason.

           bottle line   x = 0.08, i.e. 0.53 in front of each arm. Five
                         slots at a 0.15 pitch, y = -0.30 to +0.30. Three
                         are filled (whiskey, cola, beer) and two are free
                         for whatever gets added next: that should cost a
                         slot and a station name, not a re-survey.

                         The line runs ACROSS each arm's approach, not
                         along it, and that is the point of a line here.
                         bartender_pour grasps from the side, running in
                         along the arm's own +x, so slots at a common x each
                         get a clear lane and no bottle stands behind
                         another.

                         It is five slots and not nine because of
                         APPROACH_WINDOW: past y = +0.10 arm A can no longer
                         take a bottle off the line in a pose that holds,
                         and arm B picks up from y = -0.10 the other way.
                         Slots outside both windows would be tabletop, not
                         stations.

           the glass     (0.20, -0.55), arm A's own station, further out
                         than the line AND offset in y. The pour lays the
                         bottle back 0.30 behind the glass at bottle
                         height, so it sweeps across the line; the offset
                         is what puts that sweep 0.25 clear of the nearest
                         bottle instead of through it.

           the holster   (0.10, 0.40), arm B's own station, and CLOSER in
                         than the line rather than further out. Arm B
                         descends vertically onto the opener, and a holster
                         further out simply cannot be descended to: at 0.67
                         from the base, measured, arm B stopped 346mm short
                         and the goal failed outright.
    -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://bar_counter</uri>
      <name>bar_counter</name>
      <pose>0.08 0 0.9 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- ===================== the bottle line =====================

         y ascending. The free slots are y = +0.15 and +0.30; they hold
         nothing, so there is nothing to include for them. They are named in
         layout.BOTTLE_SLOTS, and that is where a new bottle gets added. -->

    <!-- Label plugins tag objects for the segmentation cameras; the numbers are
         LABEL_IDS in UR5-AlienBazaar/vlm training/vlm_labels.py (its test checks they match). -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://jack_daniels_bottle</uri>
      <name>jack_daniels_bottle</name>
      <plugin filename="gz-sim-label-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Label">
        <label>1</label>
      </plugin>
      <pose>0.08 -0.30 0.9 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <include>
      <uri>model://cola_bottle</uri>
      <name>cola_bottle</name>
      <plugin filename="gz-sim-label-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Label">
        <label>2</label>
      </plugin>
      <pose>0.08 -0.15 0.9 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- Bottle stands: shallow bottomless wells the bottle bases sit inside,
         so a bottle that is nudged during place-and-release is stopped by a
         wall 5-9mm away instead of toppling. Generated by
         bartender_gazebo/scripts/make_bottle_stands.py, which carries the
         reasoning and the dimensions.

         Posed at the bottles' OWN station poses, and bottomless, so the
         bottle bases still rest at z=0.9 exactly as before; every
         grasp_height in bartender_pour is measured up from that base and
         would otherwise be silently wrong. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://whiskey_stand</uri>
      <name>whiskey_stand</name>
      <pose>0.08 -0.30 0.9 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <include>
      <uri>model://cola_stand</uri>
      <name>cola_stand</name>
      <pose>0.08 -0.15 0.9 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- ================= the beer, and what opens it =================

         The beer takes the MIDDLE slot of the line, y = 0, which is the one
         slot equidistant from the two arms: 0.664 from each, well inside a
         UR5e's 850mm, and at +0.40 in BOTH arms' frames because they face
         each other. It has to be a place both arms can work, since one
         holds the bottle there while the other pushes down on it.

         In the old layout the holster sat 112mm from the whiskey and the
         carried opener hooked the bottle on its way past. Here they are
         0.70m apart and on opposite sides of the line.

         The beer sits in a well like the other two bottles (see
         make_bottle_stands.py). It needs one more than they do: this is the
         one bottle in the scene that gets pushed straight DOWN on while it
         is standing there. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://beer_stand</uri>
      <name>beer_stand</name>
      <pose>0.08 0.0 0.9 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <include>
      <uri>model://beer_bottle</uri>
      <name>beer_bottle</name>
      <plugin filename="gz-sim-label-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Label">
        <label>3</label>
      </plugin>
      <pose>0.08 0.0 0.9 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- The cap. A separate model on purpose: it is held to the bottle by
         the DetachableJoint declared inside beer_bottle's model.sdf, which
         is the only mechanism here that can let go of it again. The joint
         finds it by the model NAME below, so this name is load-bearing.

         Pose is the beer's station at the bottle's lip, 0.2581 above its
         base. Do not nudge it: the cap is modelled as a disc resting ON the
         lip rather than as a skirt wrapped over it, precisely so that the
         two bodies do not overlap when the joint releases them. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://beer_cap</uri>
      <name>beer_cap</name>
      <pose>0.08 0.0 1.1581 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- ============== each arm's own working station ============== -->

    <include>
      <uri>model://serving_glass</uri>
      <name>serving_glass</name>
      <plugin filename="gz-sim-label-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Label">
        <label>10</label>
      </plugin>
      <pose>0.20 -0.55 0.9 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <include>
      <uri>model://opener_holster</uri>
      <name>opener_holster</name>
      <pose>0.10 0.40 0.9 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- Opener, over the holster post. Its origin is the bell RIM, and the
         rim is what it stands on: the post is 14mm against the bell's 16mm
         depth, so it hangs inside the bell as a locator and the rim sits
         flat on the counter at 0.9. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://bottle_opener</uri>
      <name>bottle_opener</name>
      <pose>0.10 0.40 0.9 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- Simulated OAK-D depth on a stand; its pose is restated in bartender_api/config/stand_camera.yaml. -->
    <model name="oak_d_stand">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0.75 0 1.35 0 0.493051 3.141593</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <sensor name="stand_depth" type="depth_camera">
          <always_on>true</always_on>
          <update_rate>15</update_rate>
          <topic>/bartender/stand_camera/depth</topic>
          <ignition_frame_id>stand_camera_optical_frame</ignition_frame_id>
          <camera>
            <camera_info_topic>/bartender/stand_camera/camera_info</camera_info_topic>
            <horizontal_fov>1.2566</horizontal_fov>
            <image>
              <width>640</width>
              <height>400</height>
              <format>R_FLOAT32</format>
            </image>
            <clip>
              <near>0.2</near>
              <far>5.0</far>
            </clip>
            <noise>
              <type>gaussian</type>
              <mean>0.0</mean>
              <stddev>0.004</stddev>
            </noise>
          </camera>
        </sensor>
        <!-- Colour twin of stand_depth: same pose, FOV and size, so pixels line up (OAK-D aligns RGB to depth). -->
        <sensor name="stand_rgb" type="camera">
          <always_on>true</always_on>
          <update_rate>15</update_rate>
          <topic>/bartender/stand_camera/rgb</topic>
          <ignition_frame_id>stand_camera_optical_frame</ignition_frame_id>
          <camera>
            <horizontal_fov>1.2566</horizontal_fov>
            <image>
              <width>640</width>
              <height>400</height>
              <format>R8G8B8</format>
            </image>
            <clip>
              <near>0.2</near>
              <far>5.0</far>
            </clip>
          </camera>
        </sensor>
        <sensor name="bartender_stand_camera_segmentation" type="segmentation">
          <always_on>true</always_on>
          <update_rate>15</update_rate>
          <topic>/bartender/stand_camera/segmentation</topic>
          <camera>
            <segmentation_type>semantic</segmentation_type>
            <horizontal_fov>1.2566</horizontal_fov>
            <image>
              <width>640</width>
              <height>400</height>
            </image>
            <clip>
              <near>0.2</near>
              <far>5.0</far>
            </clip>
          </camera>
        </sensor>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Top-down view of arm A's work area for the VLA policy: whiskey and cola
         slots on the bottle line, and the glass. 1.1m above the counter, and the
         72 degree FOV covers about 1.6m, so arm A's base is in frame too. -->
    <model name="overhead_camera">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-0.05 -0.30 2.0 0 1.5708 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <sensor name="overhead_rgb" type="camera">
          <always_on>true</always_on>
          <update_rate>15</update_rate>
          <topic>/bartender/overhead_camera/image_raw</topic>
          <camera>
            <horizontal_fov>1.2566</horizontal_fov>
            <image>
              <width>320</width>
              <height>240</height>
              <format>R8G8B8</format>
            </image>
            <clip>
              <near>0.2</near>
              <far>3.0</far>
            </clip>
          </camera>
        </sensor>
        <sensor name="bartender_overhead_camera_segmentation" type="segmentation">
          <always_on>true</always_on>
          <update_rate>15</update_rate>
          <topic>/bartender/overhead_camera/segmentation</topic>
          <camera>
            <segmentation_type>semantic</segmentation_type>
            <horizontal_fov>1.2566</horizontal_fov>
            <image>
              <width>320</width>
              <height>240</height>
            </image>
            <clip>
              <near>0.2</near>
              <far>3.0</far>
            </clip>
          </camera>
        </sensor>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- NO PEDESTAL. Arm B stood on one, beside the counter at (0.15, 0.62),
         for as long as the counter was too small to carry both arms. It now
         bolts to the bar top the way arm A does, so the pedestal model is
         gone and so is the matching box bartender_open used to publish into
         the planning scene. The robot is one model spawned by ros_gz create
         and positioned at arm A's base; the description carries arm B from
         there. -->

  </world>
</sdf>
